### What is a Phaser?

Phaser is the most flexible synchronization barrier in java.util.concurrent. It supports dynamic party registration, multiple phases, and a callback between phases. It can replace both CountDownLatch (one-time wait) and CyclicBarrier (fixed-count reusable barrier) while adding capabilities neither provides.

```java
package com.bank.concurrency;

import java.util.concurrent.Phaser;

public class PhaserDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Phaser phaser = new Phaser(3);  // 3 initial parties

        for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
            final int workerId = i;
            new Thread(() -> {
                System.out.println("Worker-" + workerId + ": phase 0 work");
                phaser.arriveAndAwaitAdvance();  // sync at phase 0→1

                System.out.println("Worker-" + workerId + ": phase 1 work");
                phaser.arriveAndAwaitAdvance();  // sync at phase 1→2

                System.out.println("Worker-" + workerId + ": done");
                phaser.arriveAndDeregister();     // leave
            }, "Worker-" + i).start();
        }
    }
}
```

### How does dynamic registration work?

```java
package com.bank.concurrency;

import java.util.concurrent.Phaser;

public class DynamicRegistrationDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        Phaser phaser = new Phaser(1);  // main thread is party 1

        // Dynamically add workers
        for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
            phaser.register();  // add a party
            final int id = i;
            new Thread(() -> {
                System.out.println("Worker-" + id + " processing");
                phaser.arriveAndDeregister();  // done, leave
            }, "Worker-" + id).start();
        }

        phaser.arriveAndAwaitAdvance();  // main waits for all workers
        System.out.println("All workers complete");
    }
}
```

### How does onAdvance work?

```java
package com.bank.concurrency;

import java.util.concurrent.Phaser;

public class OnAdvanceDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Phaser phaser = new Phaser(3) {
            @Override
            protected boolean onAdvance(int phase, int registeredParties) {
                System.out.println("Phase " + phase + " complete. Parties: "
                        + registeredParties);
                return phase >= 2 || registeredParties == 0;
                // Terminate after phase 2 or when all parties leave
            }
        };
        // ... use phaser in worker threads
    }
}
```

### How is Phaser different from CountDownLatch and CyclicBarrier?

| Feature | CountDownLatch | CyclicBarrier | Phaser |
|---|---|---|---|
| Fixed parties | Yes (at construction) | Yes (at construction) | No (dynamic) |
| Reusable | No (one-time) | Yes (auto-reset) | Yes (auto-advance) |
| Phases | 1 (count to 0) | Multiple (trips) | Multiple (numbered) |
| Join/leave mid-run | No | No | Yes (register/deregister) |
| Callback | No | Yes (barrier action) | Yes (onAdvance) |
| Termination | count reaches 0 | reset() | onAdvance returns true |

### What is a real-world banking use case?

An ETL pipeline at National Bank of India processes data in three phases: extract, transform, and load. The number of workers varies between phases because some workers finish early or new workers are added for the heavier phases.

```java
package com.bank.etl;

import java.util.concurrent.Phaser;

public class ETLPipelineDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Phaser phaser = new Phaser(1);  // coordinator

        // Phase 0: Extract (10 workers)
        for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) startWorker(phaser, "Extract-" + i);
        phaser.arriveAndAwaitAdvance();  // wait for phase 0

        // Phase 1: Transform (5 workers, others deregistered)
        for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) startWorker(phaser, "Transform-" + i);
        phaser.arriveAndAwaitAdvance();  // wait for phase 1

        // Phase 2: Load (3 workers)
        for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) startWorker(phaser, "Load-" + i);
        phaser.arriveAndAwaitAdvance();  // wait for phase 2

        phaser.arriveAndDeregister();
        System.out.println("ETL pipeline complete");
    }

    static void startWorker(Phaser phaser, String name) {
        phaser.register();
        new Thread(() -> {
            System.out.println(name + " processing");
            phaser.arriveAndDeregister();
        }, name).start();
    }
}
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- Can a Phaser deadlock? Yes, if a registered party never arrives. Use `awaitAdvanceInterruptibly()` with a timeout to prevent permanent blocking.
- What is hierarchical tiering? Large Phasers (hundreds of parties) suffer CAS contention on the internal state. Tiering groups parties under child Phasers that register with a parent, distributing the synchronization overhead.
